\n\nFor the other use cases, testutils already has working code with issue #183 in progress, so that's off the table. That leaves format and sys as the remaining candidates, though I'd need to review the actual PRDs to understand what requirements should anchor each task. and RunDiffTests harness (rel00.0-uc004-testutils). The implementation exists in the code, so the next logical step would be writing tests for it in difftest_test.go. The code references prd001-testutils with requirements R1 through R4 covering the struct, function, environment setup, and timeout handling, which gives me a framework for what the tests should validate.\n\nBut I'm realizing that testing a differential testing harness is tricky—the harness itself gets validated through the tools that use it rather than through isolated unit tests. And I need to be careful not to duplicate issue #183 which already covers the testutils implementation. Looking at the broader rel00.0 scope, maybe I should focus on one of the other incomplete pieces like pkg/format or pkg/sys instead, since those would have more straightforward testing needs and clearer impact on the overall release. I need to be careful about anchoring tasks to specific PRD requirement IDs—I know testutils has prd001 with requirements R1 through R4, but I'm missing the actual PRD numbers for format and sys. The stitch agent can follow the required_reading references to find those PRDs, so I can still reference them even without the full contents, but I should make sure I'm being precise about which requirements map to which tasks.\n\nSince issue #183 is still in progress, I'm wondering whether I should propose test tasks for testutils now or wait until the implementation is complete. The canonical ordering suggests tests come after implementation, but the requirement IDs are already clear from the source code, so I could structure the test task proposal around R1-R4.\n\nLooking at what would deliver the most value right now, I need to wei