ing test task definition... passes when they match (prd001 R2)\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Test environment handling -- verify LC_ALL=C is set by default and DiffTest.Env overrides are applied (prd001 R3)\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Test NormalizeFunc application -- verify normalizers are applied to both reference and Go binary outputs before comparison (prd001 R4)\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Create minimal test helper programs (Go source compiled in TestMain) that produce known stdout, stderr, and exit codes to serve as mock binaries for RunDiffTests\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"All tests set LC_ALL=C per ARCHITECTURE.yaml design decision DD6\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Use testing.T subtests (t.Run) to match the subtest structure of RunDiffTests itself\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"pkg/testutils/difftest_test.go exists and compiles\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Tests verify RunDiffTests passes when both binaries produce identical stdout, stderr, and exit code\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Tests verify RunDiffTests reports divergence when stdout, stderr, or exit code differ\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"Tests verify LC_ALL=C default environment and DiffTest.Env overrides\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"Tests verify NormalizeFunc transforms are applied before comparison\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"go test ./pkg/testutils/... passes with all tests green\"\n\nI've got 6 acceptance criteria, which fits the 5-8 range for code tasks. Three design decisions is within the 3-5 target, and four requirements hits the maximum allowed. I'm reconsidering whether creating test helper programs is really an invented decision or if it's the natural consequence of needing binaries to test the comparison logic. Given the architecture requires binaries, this feels like a derived decision rather than something I'm making up. Now I'm questioning whether testing testu